  "step_3": "The decision reason hinges on avoiding lotteries with a sure outcome, but in this case, neither lottery has a sure outcome. Therefore, the reason does not provide a clear preference, and its core criterion (presence of a sure outcome) is not satisfied in either option. The individual’s reasoning, meanwhile, focuses on the probability of winning a positive amount and the relative size of the prizes, explicitly preferring Lottery B due to its higher winning chance (25% vs. 20%). There is no indication that the individual considered whether either lottery involved a certain outcome or used the absence of certainty as a basis for preference. The information considered (probabilities of positive outcomes, prize amounts) and the justification (favoring higher chance of winning) are unrelated to the decision reason’s focus on sure outcomes. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual used this reason. The absence of sure outcomes in both lotteries further diminishes the relevance of the reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
